1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,720
After the last light, Alaska folds itself into white.

2
00:00:03,720 --> 00:00:10,527
The Brooks Range glows lavender for ten minutes, then gives way to the deepest quiet of the year.

3
00:00:10,527 --> 00:00:20,984
Tonight we follow four of its citizens — the wolf, the caribou, the eagle, and the raven — as each one prepares, in its own way, to outlast the dark.

4
00:00:20,984 --> 00:00:24,633
Listen: the wind does most of the talking from here.

5
00:00:44,630 --> 00:00:53,108
At dawn, the sign reads like handwriting in the snow — track upon track, spacing steady, the gait of tireless travelers.

6
00:00:53,108 --> 00:01:04,201
A wolf pack passed here before the storm: eight, maybe nine strong, each step half a thought for the lead and half for the young one limping at the very end.

7
00:01:04,201 --> 00:01:08,228
Wolves move like weather — patient, indifferent, certain.

8
00:01:08,228 --> 00:01:13,103
By midday they had folded themselves out of sight on the white slope.

9
00:01:37,100 --> 00:01:38,831
And now — silence again.

10
00:01:38,831 --> 00:01:45,467
The pack has found its resting hollow, breath rising in slow silver streams above the drift.

11
00:01:45,467 --> 00:01:51,670
The white one sleeps first, always; the elder keeps one ear raised, counting the wind.

12
00:01:51,670 --> 00:01:56,430
No fire burns warmer than a wolf at rest, and none sleeps lighter.

13
00:01:56,430 --> 00:02:02,344
They dream of the herd, and the herd, somewhere on the long plain, dreams of them.

14
00:02:02,344 --> 00:02:06,383
It is all very calm, out here, if you watch long enough.

15
00:02:30,380 --> 00:02:34,284
Farther north, the caribou have found the frozen river again.

16
00:02:34,284 --> 00:02:41,901
Six thousand hooves make almost no sound at all — a dry whisper and a clicking of bone, like faint rain inside the sky.

17
00:02:41,901 --> 00:02:47,021
Drifted six-deep behind the lead cow, they draw one slow line across the tundra.

18
00:02:47,021 --> 00:02:54,125
The calves travel warm at the center, learning the shape of a map they will follow for the rest of their lives.

19
00:03:18,130 --> 00:03:20,208
I can hardly believe the distance.

20
00:03:20,208 --> 00:03:28,399
I have been standing here since first light, and the herd is still passing — a living river with no visible source and no visible end.

21
00:03:28,399 --> 00:03:35,062
My lens iced over twice already, and I would not trade this frozen morning for any summer I have ever filmed.

22
00:03:35,062 --> 00:03:36,407
They just keep coming…

23
00:03:36,407 --> 00:03:39,524
and somehow, that is the most restful thing I know.

24
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:08,128
And look how gently it moves — the whole herd like one long exhale.

25
00:04:08,128 --> 00:04:11,910
There was never any hurry, even before the winter came.

26
00:04:11,910 --> 00:04:20,370
Each step is a saving of energy, quietly banked against the dark months ahead, when even the moonlight will seem to freeze.

27
00:04:20,370 --> 00:04:22,433
Rest now: the river will wait.

28
00:04:22,433 --> 00:04:31,236
The river has never once run dry in their memory — a memory that has survived a thousand winters, and still believes the spring.

29
00:04:55,240 --> 00:05:01,165
Higher still, above the frozen creek-bed, the bald eagle has already made its choices.

30
00:05:01,165 --> 00:05:06,469
On a post of bleached spruce it folds its wings; the work of the day is done.

31
00:05:06,469 --> 00:05:14,461
The beak turns once toward the creek, where the sleeping salmon lie silver and still beneath the ice's first breath.

32
00:05:14,461 --> 00:05:15,563
But not tonight.

33
00:05:15,563 --> 00:05:21,832
The talons withdraw, the head bows to the shoulder, and the king of the Alaskan sky simply…

34
00:05:21,832 --> 00:05:23,348
disappears into sleep.

35
00:05:47,350 --> 00:05:52,227
Below the eagle's post, a raven has arrived to have the last word.

36
00:05:52,227 --> 00:05:56,218
It knows the leaning-posts better than the eagle does.

37
00:05:56,218 --> 00:06:01,169
It croaks once — a dry, deliberate laugh — then minds its own coat.

38
00:06:01,169 --> 00:06:08,928
Ravens keep the winter's true diary: which carcass, which fox, which way the wind means to blow tomorrow.

39
00:06:08,928 --> 00:06:18,018
Satisfied with today's entries, it steps off smartly, envelopes the falling snow, and is gone before anyone hears it leave.

40
00:06:42,020 --> 00:06:44,415
That is all, from Alaska tonight.

41
00:06:44,415 --> 00:06:53,921
Wolf, caribou, eagle, raven — each out there now, breathing the same thin northern air, holding the same delicate thread of warmth.

42
00:06:53,921 --> 00:07:00,380
The aurora has begun to flicker above the ridge, like a candle somebody keeps relighting.

43
00:07:00,380 --> 00:07:05,823
Let the tail of the wind carry you down, gently, into your own quiet place.

44
00:07:05,823 --> 00:07:06,621
Sleep well.

45
00:07:06,621 --> 00:07:12,427
The wild north is watching over all of this — and it asks for nothing in return.
